<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Tool permissions · Agentic Bible</title><meta name="description" content="The boundaries that determine who may request a tool action, which resource it may affect, and where authorization is enforced."><meta property="og:title" content="Tool permissions · Agentic Bible"><meta property="og:description" content="The boundaries that determine who may request a tool action, which resource it may affect, and where authorization is enforced."><meta property="og:type" content="article"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Agentic Bible"><meta property="og:url" content="https://agenticbible.si/articles/tool-permissions/"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary"><meta name="twitter:title" content="Tool permissions · Agentic Bible"><meta name="twitter:description" content="The boundaries that determine who may request a tool action, which resource it may affect, and where authorization is enforced."><meta name="robots" content="index, follow"><meta name="color-scheme" content="light"><link rel="canonical" href="https://agenticbible.si/articles/tool-permissions/"><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"TechArticle","headline":"Tool permissions","description":"The boundaries that determine who may request a tool action, which resource it may affect, and where authorization is enforced.","articleSection":"Protocols","inLanguage":"en","url":"https://agenticbible.si/articles/tool-permissions/","mainEntityOfPage":{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://agenticbible.si/articles/tool-permissions/"},"citation":["https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/basic/authorization","https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8707","https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2025-11-25/tutorials/security/security_best_practices"]}</script><link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/_astro/Base.DyzAbnuc.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/_astro/_id_.w1YKO1jA.css"></head><body><a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header shell"><a class="wordmark" href="/"><img src="/favicon.svg" width="36" height="36" alt=""><span>Agentic Bible<span class="wordmark-sub">A living reference</span></span></a><nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="/" aria-current="page">Library</a><a href="/topics/">Topics</a><a href="/about/">About</a><a href="/history/">History</a></nav><a class="header-search" href="/search/" aria-label="Search the library"><svg width="17" height="17" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="8.5" cy="8.5" r="5.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"></circle><path d="m13 13 4 4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"></path></svg><span>Search</span><kbd aria-hidden="true">/</kbd></a></header><div class="preview-strip" role="region" aria-label="Edition status"><div class="shell"><span class="status-dot"></span> Preview edition <span class="strip-detail">Sourced working drafts. Editorial review pending.</span></div></div><main id="main" class="shell" tabindex="-1"><div class="article-breadcrumb" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="/" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Library</a><span data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>/</span><a href="/topics/protocols/" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Protocols</a></div><div class="reading-layout" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><article class="article" data-pagefind-body data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><header id="overview" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><p class="eyebrow" data-pagefind-meta="topic" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Protocols / Reference entry 11</p><h1 data-pagefind-meta="title" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Tool permissions</h1><p class="article-deck" data-pagefind-meta="description" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>The boundaries that determine who may request a tool action, which resource it may affect, and where authorization is enforced.</p><div class="article-status" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><span class="draft-pill" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><span class="status-dot" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2></span> Working draft</span><span data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Human editorial certification pending</span></div><nav class="article-actions" aria-label="Article actions" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="#sources" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Sources (3)</a><a href="#revision" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Revision history</a><a href="#share" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Share or cite</a><a href="/contribute/?article=tool-permissions" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Contribution options</a></nav></header><aside class="contents" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><details data-article-contents data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><summary data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>On this page</summary><nav aria-label="On this page" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="#overview" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Overview</a><a href="#identity-resource-and-action-are-different-questions" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Identity, resource and action are different questions</a><a href="#validate-at-the-receiver" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Validate at the receiver</a><a href="#descriptions-are-not-enforcement" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Descriptions are not enforcement</a><a href="#check-changes-and-failures" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Check changes and failures</a><a href="#sources" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Sources & context</a><a href="#revision" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Revision record</a></nav></details><div class="contents-bottom" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><span class="eyebrow" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Part of the collection</span><a href="/topics/protocols/" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Protocols <svg class="icon-arrow" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><path d="M3 13 13 3M3 3h10v10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2></path></svg></a></div></aside><div class="prose" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><p><strong>Tool permissions</strong> determine which operations an <a href="/articles/ai-agent/">agent</a> may cause and which resources those operations may affect. <a href="/articles/tool-use/">Tool use</a> explains the request/execution boundary; permission checking belongs at that boundary and any downstream resource server. A model’s ability to describe or select a tool does not establish access. MCP’s authorization specification gives a concrete protocol example by assigning distinct roles to the client, resource owner, authorization server and protected resource server. <a href="#source-mcp-authorization">1</a></p>
<h2 id="identity-resource-and-action-are-different-questions">Identity, resource and action are different questions</h2>
<p>A useful permission question identifies the actor, the intended resource and the permitted operation. RFC 8707 introduces a resource indicator so a client can identify where a requested access token will be used. The RFC distinguishes that resource from the scope of access and explains how the authorization server can restrict a token’s audience. These are protocol mechanisms; they do not establish whether the user wanted a particular agent action. <a href="#source-resource-indicators">2</a></p>
<p>For an illustrative reference-library application, permission to read an article would be different from permission to replace its current revision. The resource would also matter: a grant concerning one collection would not, by itself, grant authority over another. This is an original design example, not a configured authorization service on this site.</p>
<p>A human decision to allow a specific edit would answer another question: whether that proposed change should proceed now. A reusable credential and an action-specific confirmation serve different purposes. A design that combines them would still need to bind the decision to the actual operation being executed.</p>
<h2 id="validate-at-the-receiver">Validate at the receiver</h2>
<p>The versioned MCP HTTP authorization specification requires resource servers to validate tokens, including that a token was issued for the server as its intended audience. It also describes conveying authorization with each HTTP request and selecting access scopes. Its authorization flow is for HTTP-based transports; the document treats local stdio credentials differently. A protocol version and transport therefore belong in any claim about the mechanism. <a href="#source-mcp-authorization">1</a></p>
<p>RFC 8707 explains why audience restriction matters when a token presented to one resource must not become a credential for unrelated resources. It also discusses identifying the intended tenant within a resource URI where relevant. A broad hostname alone may not express the intended boundary in a multi-tenant system. <a href="#source-resource-indicators">2</a></p>
<p>The security guidance for <a href="/articles/model-context-protocol/">MCP</a> separately prohibits token passthrough and discusses a confused deputy: a component with its own authority can be induced to misuse that authority for another party. A working connection is consequently insufficient evidence that every delegated action is correctly authorized. <a href="#source-mcp-security">3</a></p>
<h2 id="descriptions-are-not-enforcement">Descriptions are not enforcement</h2>
<p>In the library example, a tool description could say that it updates only draft articles. The application would still need to check the actual target and its current state. Similarly, calling a field “read-only” would not constrain a server that implements a write behind that name. These are reasons to examine the execution contract rather than infer permissions from interface labels.</p>
<p>An external document asking the agent to change the target collection would remain untrusted input. <a href="/articles/prompt-injection/">Prompt injection</a> concerns that attempted redirection; authorization limits what a redirected component can successfully do. <a href="/articles/agent-sandboxing/">Sandboxing</a> can restrict an execution environment, but it does not decide whether a permitted application-level edit is factually justified.</p>
<h2 id="check-changes-and-failures">Check changes and failures</h2>
<p>For the example, useful tests would include an expired grant, a valid grant for a different resource, an operation outside the allowed scope, and a resource whose state changed after the proposal. A denial should remain distinguishable from a network failure or an unknown outcome. Those tests would inspect actual receiver behavior, rather than accept an agent’s summary of its authority.</p>
<p>Permission design is specific to a system and its threat model. The cited protocol documents provide requirements and mechanisms, not a certification of this encyclopedia. <a href="/articles/human-oversight/">Human oversight</a> and recorded action outcomes can make decisions reviewable, while enforcement still depends on the application and services that carry out the request.</p>
</div><p class="article-feedback" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="/contribute/?article=tool-permissions" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Suggest a correction or primary source</a> · Public intake remains on hold; the local synthetic form covers only the original three entries.</p><section class="sources" id="sources" aria-labelledby="sources-title" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><p class="eyebrow" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Follow the evidence</p><h2 id="sources-title" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Sources & context</h2><p data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Primary sources checked on <time datetime="2026-10-17" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>17 October 2026</time>. A source check is not independent editorial approval.</p><ol data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><li id="source-mcp-authorization" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/basic/authorization" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Model Context Protocol authorization <span aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><svg class="icon-arrow" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><path d="M3 13 13 3M3 3h10v10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2></path></svg></span></a><span class="source-publisher" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Model Context Protocol</span><p data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Versioned 25 November 2025 specification, checked 17 October 2026; HTTP authorization roles, resource parameters, scope and access-token validation. It does not define every application permission.</p></li><li id="source-resource-indicators" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8707" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>RFC 8707: Resource Indicators for OAuth 2.0 <span aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><svg class="icon-arrow" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><path d="M3 13 13 3M3 3h10v10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2></path></svg></span></a><span class="source-publisher" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>IETF</span><p data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>February 2020 Standards Track RFC; sections 2–3 distinguish the intended resource from scope and explain audience restrictions. Describes an OAuth mechanism, not agent behavior or user approval of individual actions.</p></li><li id="source-mcp-security" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2025-11-25/tutorials/security/security_best_practices" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Model Context Protocol security best practices <span aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><svg class="icon-arrow" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><path d="M3 13 13 3M3 3h10v10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2></path></svg></span></a><span class="source-publisher" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Model Context Protocol</span><p data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Versioned security guidance checked 17 October 2026; token passthrough and confused-deputy risks. These requirements do not prove that a particular deployment enforces them.</p></li></ol></section><section class="revision" id="revision" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><h2 data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Revision record</h2><dl data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><div data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><dt data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Authorship</dt><dd data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>AI-assisted project draft</dd></div><div data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><dt data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Source check</dt><dd data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><time datetime="2026-10-17" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>17 October 2026</time>. This records source consultation, not editorial certification.</dd></div><div data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><dt data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Human editorial review</dt><dd data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Pending. No human editorial certification is recorded.</dd></div><div data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><dt data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Latest source change</dt><dd data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><time datetime="2026-10-17" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>17 October 2026</time> · Add six sourced entries on agent operating principles · <code data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>54b9d079</code></dd></div><div data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><dt data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Revision receipt</dt><dd data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>No separate publication receipt is recorded for this working draft.</dd></div></dl><p class="review-scope" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>The twenty entries received deep reading across external and internal review. Claim verification was sampled; this does not certify every factual claim.</p><a class="text-link" data-source-commit="8a38252384c6fe6024a75b462ad95294a131100c" href="https://github.com/Leshey-Digital/agentic-bible/commits/8a38252384c6fe6024a75b462ad95294a131100c/src/content/articles/tool-permissions.md" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>View file history on GitHub <svg class="icon-arrow" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><path d="M3 13 13 3M3 3h10v10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2></path></svg></a></section><section class="share-tools" id="share" aria-labelledby="share-title" data-pagefind-ignore data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><p class="eyebrow" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Keep a useful reference</p><h2 id="share-title" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Share or cite this entry</h2><p id="article-citation" tabindex="0" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>“Tool permissions.” Agentic Bible. https://agenticbible.si/articles/tool-permissions/</p><div class="share-buttons" data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2><button type="button" data-copy-link hidden data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Copy article link</button><button type="button" data-copy-citation hidden data-astro-cid-poxfbvb2>Copy citation</button></div><p class="share-status" 